    Negative-direction witness — using Shared instead of
    MaybeShared rejected:

      Take Row A6 (uniqueness_maybeshared_not_shared). Suppose
      hypothetical implementer sets uniqueness to Shared
      (strict lattice TOP) in IC-8a's CONSERVATIVE seed:

        pub const CONSERVATIVE_WRONG: ParamContract = ParamContract {
            access: Access::Owned,
            consumption: Consumption::Unrestricted,
            cardinality: Cardinality::Many,
            locality: Locality::Unknown,
            uniqueness: Uniqueness::Shared, // WRONG — should be MaybeShared
            may_share: true,
        };

      Then non-enumerable function parameters would receive
      Shared uniqueness. Per DP-9 (`Shared ⟹ StaticShared`),
      downstream consumers would emit unconditional copy for
      all mutations on the parameter — losing the
      optimization opportunity when callers actually hold
      unique references (which the runtime IsShared check
      would have detected under MaybeShared).

      Per Annex E §AIMS §1 ("RC rare, not RC fast"):
      this is a precision regression. The MaybeShared seed
      (per the IC-8a spec rule line) is the maximally-precise
      sound seed — Shared is sound but unnecessarily
      pessimistic. The deviation from strict lattice TOP is
      intentional per (P3) rationale; reverting to strict TOP
      loses optimization potential without soundness benefit.

    IC-8a dependencies — supporting role within AIMS contract
    layer:

      IC-8a consumes the address-taken / closure classification
      (computed at `analyze_program()` initialization) and
      produces the per-parameter CONSERVATIVE seed for
      non-enumerable functions. The dependency chain at the
      proof-obligation level is acyclic:

        IC-1 (SCC decomposition)
          --> address-taken classification (per-function
                                              non-enumerable
                                              flag)
        address-taken classification --> IC-8a (CONSERVATIVE
                                                  seed for
                                                  non-enumerable
                                                  functions)
        IC-2 (OPTIMISTIC seeding for enumerable functions)
          --xor-- IC-8a (CONSERVATIVE seeding for non-enumerable
                          functions)
          ; binary classification ensures mutual exclusion
        IC-8a + IC-2 --> IC-3 (ParamContract componentwise
                                 max-join + may_share OR;
                                 consumes both seed types
                                 uniformly via the same join
                                 operator)
        L-1 / L-2 / L-6 (§AIMS §3 lattice properties) --> IC-8a
                                                          (TOP-
                                                          preservation
                                                          under
                                                          max-
                                                          join)
        IC-8a --> Step 5 emission (consumes CONSERVATIVE
                                     contracts to emit
                                     conservative ABI + runtime
                                     checks)
        IC-8a --> RL-29 / RL-30 (LLVM fact-export gates
                                   consume CONSERVATIVE
                                   contracts to suppress
                                   `noalias` annotations +
                                   emit conservative `memory`
                                   attributes)
        IC-8a --> VF-6 (contract-realization agreement
                          verification at sec-9 VF-6)

      IC-8a is the address-taken / closure CONSERVATIVE-
      seeding operator within the IC layer; the
      classification is computed once at `analyze_program()`
      initialization; L-* lemmas are proven upstream in sec-02.

    Algorithm-citation declaration:
      algorithm: per-function binary classification
                           (address-taken / non-enumerable XOR
                           enumerable / known call sites) via
                           function-value-flow analysis
                           detecting `PartialApply` captures,
                           higher-order arguments, public-API
                           exports, and trait-object dispatch
                           tables; CONSERVATIVE seed
                           initialization for non-enumerable
                           cases (lattice TOP per dim except
                           uniqueness = MaybeShared); IC-2
                           OPTIMISTIC seed initialization for
                           enumerable cases; SCC fixpoint via
                           IC-3 max-join + OR-monoid uniformly
                           applied to both seed types
      published basis: Cousot & Cousot, "Abstract
                           Interpretation: A Unified Lattice
                           Model for Static Analysis of
                           Programs by Construction or
                           Approximation of Fixpoints", POPL
                           1977 (interprocedural summary
                           computation via SCC-topological
                           monotone iteration with
                           context-insensitive widening at
                           non-enumerable call site
                           boundaries); Reynolds, J. C.,
                           "Definitional Interpreters for
                           Higher-Order Programming
                           Languages", Higher-Order and
                           Symbolic Computation, vol. 11,
                           no. 4, 1998 (function-value-flow
                           analysis for higher-order
                           programs); Tarski, A., "A lattice-
                           theoretical fixpoint theorem and
                           its applications", Pacific Journal
                           of Mathematics, vol. 5, no. 2,
                           pp. 285-309, 1955 (least-fixpoint
                           theorem on complete lattices —
                           lattice TOP is the maximally-
                           conservative starting point for
                           bottom-up fixpoint iteration);
                           Lorenzen, A., Leijen, D., Reinking,
                           A., "FP²: Fully In-Place
                           Functional Programming Provides
                           Memory Reuse for Pure Functional
                           Programs", ICFP 2023 (Section 6
                           Theorem 5: address-taken /
                           closure handling for FBIP
                           certification with sound
                           conservative seeding)
      complexity: O(N) per `is_address_taken`
                           classification (single pass over
                           IR detecting `PartialApply` +
                           higher-order arg + public-API +
                           trait-object dispatch); O(n) per
                           CONSERVATIVE seed initialization
                           (Rust `vec![CONSERVATIVE; n]`
                           constant-time per parameter);
                           O(scc_size × iteration_count) per
                           Step 1 fixpoint loop (IC-7 bound)
      determinism: deterministic; classification is a
                           pure total function of the IR's
                           function-value-flow graph (no
                           hash-map iteration order
                           dependency); CONSERVATIVE seed is
                           a compile-time constant; SCC
                           topological order is consistent
                           across compilation runs per IC-1
      reference impl: classification at 
                           compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/
                           interprocedural/mod.rs
                           (`is_address_taken` per-function
                           predicate); `ParamContract::
                           CONSERVATIVE` constant at
                           compiler/ori_arc/
                           src/aims/contract/mod.rs; IC-8a
                           seeding applied at SCC fixpoint
                           initialization in
                           interprocedural/mod.rs
                           `analyze_program()`
      cross-language Lean 4
      prior art: `lean4/src/Lean/Compiler/IR/Borrow.lean`
                           applies similar address-taken
                           classification for borrow inference
                           with conservative seeding when
                           call sites are non-enumerable;
                           Koka Perceus
                           (`koka/src/Core/Borrowed.hs`)
                           uses similar binary classification
                           (visible / non-visible call sites)
                           with conservative borrowing for
                           non-visible cases; Swift
                           SILOptimizer's
                           `FunctionOrderInfo` distinguishes
                           "may-have-unknown-callers" from
                           "all-callers-known" with similar
                           seeding strategies; FP² paper's
                           Section 6 formalizes the closure /
                           address-taken handling that AIMS
                           implements; the published reference-compiler corpus
                           cites Lean 4 + Swift ARC + Koka
                           Perceus as the prior art set

